/T (*Amino Acids: Essential and Conditionally Essential** Amino acids are categorized based on their necessity and ability to be synthesized by the human body. Indispensable \(or essential\) amino acids cannot be produced by the body in sufficient quantities to meet the demands of human growth and maintenance. The intake requirements for these amino acids are defined as the minimal level necessary to achieve nitrogen balance or offset the irreversible oxidative loss of the amino acid without major changes in protein turnover, provided there is an energy balance with moderate physical activity. For specific groups like infants, children, and pregnant or lactating women, these requirements also account for protein deposition and milk protein secretion.)
/E (*Amino Acids: Essential and Conditionally Essential** Amino acids are categorized based on their necessity and ability to be synthesized by the human body. Indispensable \(or essential\) amino acids cannot be produced by the body in sufficient quantities to meet the demands of human growth and maintenance. The intake requirements for these amino acids are defined as the minimal level necessary to achieve nitrogen balance or offset the irreversible oxidative loss of the amino acid without major changes in protein turnover, provided there is an energy balance with moderate physical activity. For specific groups like infants, children, and pregnant or lactating women, these requirements also account for protein deposition and milk protein secretion.)
/Pg 24 0 R
/K [1]
