/T (*Protein Digestibility and Quality** Historically, protein digestibility was assessed by comparing nitrogen intake to fecal nitrogen output, which underestimated the "true" digestibility because fecal nitrogen included endogenous sources. Tracer techniques have shown that the true digestibility of most dietary proteins is high. The quality of food protein can be measured using the protein digestibility-corrected amino acid score. Typically, animal protein sources have higher concentrations of indispensable amino acids compared to plant-based proteins, with lysine often being the most limiting amino acid, followed by sulfur amino acids \(methionine and cystine\), tryptophan, and threonine.)
/E (*Protein Digestibility and Quality** Historically, protein digestibility was assessed by comparing nitrogen intake to fecal nitrogen output, which underestimated the "true" digestibility because fecal nitrogen included endogenous sources. Tracer techniques have shown that the true digestibility of most dietary proteins is high. The quality of food protein can be measured using the protein digestibility-corrected amino acid score. Typically, animal protein sources have higher concentrations of indispensable amino acids compared to plant-based proteins, with lysine often being the most limiting amino acid, followed by sulfur amino acids \(methionine and cystine\), tryptophan, and threonine.)
