/E (It's important to understand that the body's reactions to different chemicals can differ based on their structure. While some reactions to toxins are immunological, others are of a different nature. For example, certain individuals experience asthma when exposed to plicatic acid found in cedar. This asthma is triggered by an antigen-antibody reaction initiated by the immune system. Plicatic acid acts as an antigen, prompting our bodies to produce antibodies to counteract its effects. Conversely, some people suffer asthma attacks upon contact with formaldehyde without any antigen-antibody reaction; their symptoms stem from a different mechanism.)
/Pg 39 0 R
/K [2]
>>
endobj
403 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 187 0 R
/E (Our bodies absorb various chemicals that tend to remain in tissues for extended periods. Most of these chemicals are lipophilic, meaning they dissolve easily in fat, a key component of cell membranes. Lipophilic, or lipid-soluble, substances are complex, making it challenging for the body to break them down and eliminate them. In contrast, water-soluble chemicals can be excreted without alteration or through simple metabolic changes. For a chemical to be converted from lipid-soluble and non-polar \(uncharged\) to water-soluble and polar \(charged\), it must undergo two chemical phases, facilitated by specific enzymes.)
/Pg 39 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
