1080 0 obj
<<
/Title (Hair It may come as a surprise to many, but furry animals have the remarkable ability to excrete xenobiotics in significant quantities through their hair. In essence, their hair acts as an excretory tissue for both essential and non-essential potentially toxic substances. These expelled elements become permanently integrated into their growing hair, with the amount excreted correlating to the levels of these components found in other tissues of their body. Consequently, animal hair can be utilized to assess the exposure to toxic substances. Interestingly, in many instances, the hair of these animals can reveal more information than their blood. This is primarily because blood tests indicate the concentration of waste materials being eliminated from the body, rather than the concentration of toxins and waste that remain within it. Hair serves as a historical record of all substances accumulated in the body, containing trace elements approximately 200 times more than what is found in the blood. While blood reflects recent exposure to toxic substances, hair indicates chronic exposure over an extended period. Hair grows at an average rate of about 0.5 inches per month; therefore, if an individual's hair measures five inches long, it captures a record of all the toxins they have likely been exposed to over the past ten months.)
/Dest [34 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 7 0 R
/Prev 1082 0 R
/Next 1078 0 R
>>
endobj
